		<meta property="schema:genre">Nonfiction</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Philosophy</meta>
		<meta property="schema:abstract">The conservative political theorist Edmund Burke critiques the political program of the French Revolution.</meta>
		<dc:description>
			<p>Written in 1790, during the early stages of the French Revolution, <i>Reflections on the Revolution in France</i> is regarded as one of the foundational texts of conservative political theory. In his <i>Reflections</i>, the British philosopher and statesman <a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a> critiqued the political program of the French Revolution, dismissing it as a political order founded on abstract principles. Instead, Burke advocated constitutional rule based on received institutions, incremental reform, and sensitivity to the vagaries of human nature. Some of Burke’s predictions, such as the revolutionary government becoming a “mischevious and ignoble oligarchy” and the politicization of the military bringing about a “military republic,” proved to be prescient in the face of subsequent events like the Reign of Terror and Napoleon’s coup of 18 Brumaire.</p>
		</dc:description>
